    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work when called through a real client over stdio.\n\n**What changed from older examples:** in the pinned mcp 2.2.0, `FastMCP` no longer exists; it was renamed. The server now uses `MCPServer(\"notes\")` from `mcp.server.mcpserver`, with `@server.tool()` for each tool. `python -m app.server` still calls `build_server().run()`, which serves over stdio by default.\n\n**Error handling:** for an unknown title, `word_count` raises `ToolError(f\"no such note: {title!r}\")`. In this version, only `ToolError` passes its message to the model. Any other exception, such as `KeyError` or `ValueError`, reaches the model only as \"Error executing tool word_count\", so it wouldn't know why the call failed.\n\n**How I checked it:**\n- **Save:** `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` returned `saved a`.\n- **Count:** `word_count(\"a\")` returned `4`, so repeated spaces and newlines are handled correctly.\n- **Unknown title:** `word_count(\"zz\")` returned `is_error=True` with the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zz'`.\n- **Smoke test:** `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- **Tests:** `pytest -q` shows 1 passed.\n\nThe tests only check that `NOTES` starts empty; nothing in the test suite exercises the tools, so the stdio run above was the real check.\n\nI wrote that check as a scratch file, `_check.py`, in the project root. Deleting it needed an approval that wasn't given, so it's still there and you can delete it."
  },
